%% How a rule (DATA) becomes a runnable matcher — and why data can never execute.
%% Source: src/core/compile.ts, ruleset.ts, matchers/builtins.ts, ADR-005
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Dev as Contributor
    participant Spec as RuleSpec (data)
    participant Comp as compileRule()
    participant Reg as Closed builtin registry
    participant Rule as Rule (runnable)

    Dev->>Spec: author id · severity · tier · framework<br/>matcher · pass/fail fixtures · precisionBudget
    Spec->>Comp: compile at load time
    alt matcher.kind == "line-pattern"
        Comp->>Comp: new RegExp(pattern, flags)<br/>(MATCHES text — never executes it)
        Comp-->>Rule: detect(file) → RuleMatch[]
    else matcher.kind == "builtin"
        Comp->>Reg: look up name (must already exist)
        Reg-->>Comp: vetted, code-reviewed function
        Comp-->>Rule: detect(file) [+ detectCrossFile]
    end
    Note over Spec,Reg: Data selects from a CLOSED vocabulary.<br/>No eval, no Function, no dynamic import.<br/>A rule cannot define new behaviour.
    Note over Rule: precision-budget guard: a rule that<br/>regresses corpus false-positives is reverted, not merged.
